docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-1.txt
**The plan is not ready to approve:** its measurement can miss lost questions and false merges, and several proposed rules go beyond the signed standard. For example, the stored week has a total-revenue question and a revenue-growth question that bypass the proposed same-type comparison; the plan’s own gold construction can miss that pair.

Findings are ranked by severity.

1. **The proposed gold can miss both false merges and missing required questions.** [Plan lines 100–101](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:100) builds G1 from listed pairs plus unlisted pairs with the same object **and type**, and G2 from questions that already opened. In the v38 ledger, `v4` asks FY2027 total revenue and `v10` asks FY2027 revenue growth; match record 14 calls them different quantities. [D212](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2707) confirms that revenue/growth bypasses the same-measure gate. The pair can therefore be absent from G1 even if a future path merges it incorrectly. A required question never proposed or opened cannot enter G2 at all. The existing admission gold provides some missing-question coverage, but only for its specific cases ([D188](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2483)). A gold derived from source material, including required questions absent from the ledger and relevant cross-type pairs, is needed to detect these losses.

2. **The acceptance conditions allow material regressions to pass.** [Plan lines 124–126](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:124) permit false merges up to the control’s count, even if the new arm loses a different required question. They count evidence as preserved if it is attached *anywhere*, even if it is attached to the wrong question. [D212](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2705) already records three material pieces under the wrong product question and says reducing page count did not restore dropped material. The evidence-dedup arm also has no stated acceptance threshold for G3 or a check that a different or richer claim remains visible to the judge; that was the loss behind [D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2611).

3. **H7’s proposed identity key can hide distinct evidence.** [Plan line 92](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:92) treats equal speaker, date and numbers as one statement. One speaker can make two different claims about the same number on the same day, or add a qualification. Accurate fields do not establish equal meaning. The proposed single display also conflicts with the decision to show both source records to the judge, labelled as an opinion ([D201](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2587), [D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2612)). The plan names those decisions, but does not state this conflict.

4. **The claimed cause of the ACP split is unproven.** [Plan lines 12–16 and 68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:12) attribute the split to the “guarantee beside a financing” sentence. The sentence **is** in both [the collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1) and [the signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318); the plan is correct on that factual point. But v38 match records 6 and 10 record only the verdicts, not the instruction that caused them. The plan itself acknowledges that the model account has not been obtained ([lines 75–76](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:75)). The two standard lines create a real boundary to resolve, but the records do not establish that this sentence caused the split or that every guarantee and financing within a plan must be separate.

5. **H1, H3 and the general rule add boundaries that the standard does not settle.** [Plan lines 80–82 and 86–88](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:80) add “when uncertain, merge,” “one plan, one question,” and “one product generation, one question.” The signed standard does say one outcome can settle two questions ([line 316](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:316)), so that part is correctly attributed. It also requires separation where answers can move apart ([line 274](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:274)), and [D236](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2871) permits a launch **or** shipment question; it does not decide that all milestones are one question. D243’s caution about noisy extraction is not a decision to merge uncertain questions ([standard lines 153–156](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:153)). The v38 ledger’s `v11` launch and `v17` shipment are a concrete case requiring that distinction.

6. **H4 treats different periods as a settled duplicate.** [Plan line 89](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:89) says FY2027 CPU revenue and calendar-2026 server CPU revenue are the same question under the standard. They differ in both scope and period. The standard’s duplicate test concerns another presentation of the **same result** ([line 289](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:289)); it separately treats the next period as succession ([line 317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:317)). The earlier [Claude-labelled review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:30) labels this pair duplicate, but is not an Alex-approved decision.

7. **H2 and H5 do not fully cover the existing identity contract.** H2’s proposed merge keeps the earlier question’s fixed outcomes and due date; the plan does not show how a later deadline remains answerable after the earlier deadline passes. The existing question and outcomes are immutable ([PRD 06 lines 8 and 14](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:8)); succession is continued tracking after an event question closes ([line 176](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:176)). H5 says code would retain only object, type and due year ([plan line 90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:90)); that omits scope, event type and other distinctions currently used by [identity.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:18). It could coalesce separate events, and the plan does not enumerate that risk.

8. **M3’s cited mechanism and proposed repair do not cover all the cited misses.** [Plan lines 70 and 148](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:70) combine peers missed by retrieval with peers excluded by type, but propose sending only same-object, same-type questions to the judge. That cannot cover the revenue/growth case above. The earlier review says four twins were outside the top ten, while also saying collision judging ran on all 11 candidates ([report lines 40–46](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:40)); those statements do not establish that the judge saw each missing twin. The actual gate requires both membership in the list and equal measure ([jev_matching.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:195)).

9. **Several counts are presented more strongly than their sources allow.** The 69 questions, 80 candidates, 1 merge, 2 successions, and v38’s 17 questions, 19 candidates and 0 merges match the [census output](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission-census.txt:1). The 24/12/10/4 labels match the earlier [50-question review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:58), but they are Claude’s unapproved judgments; 12 + 10 is 22 of 50, with four additional borderline cases. Thus [plan lines 8–10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:8) cannot establish that half should not exist or that the two causes are roughly equal. The 12 and 3 M1 counts are *verdict categories*, not verified duplicate errors. Further, the census classifies by parsing `reason` text ([script lines 19–34](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:19)), contrary to the required field-based census ([AGENTS.md line 52](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:52)); the counts should be checked against the underlying records before being used as a mechanism census.

10. **“Different statements” is a model-label count, not a verified fact count.** The 1,888/1,168, 1,863/1,123, 52/27 and 46/29 figures match the stored census. The script collapses connected `same_claims` labels transitively ([week_counts.py lines 12–18](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/week_counts.py:12)); [D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2611) records eight incorrect or richer merges in 40 sampled labels. They are therefore counts *after collapsing matcher-labelled pairs*, not established counts of distinct claims. I independently verified that `v1` and `v3` share 20 evidence IDs in the v38 ledger ([plan line 62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:62)).

11. **The gold and experiment sequence needs clearer controls.** [Plan lines 104–109](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:104) says both to label and resolve disagreements on the gold and that the holdout is not read before changes. It must distinguish reading source material to create a frozen holdout from exposing its labels or outcomes to the people tuning the arms. The plan also proposes directly using old admission gold ([line 112](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:112)), while its [standard comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/gold-check.md:3) says admission `must_not_open` labels need re-derivation for ledger-blind extraction. Finally, the plan schedules a rewrite addressing M1 **and M4** together ([line 145](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:145)), so a regression cannot be attributed to one change as required by [AGENTS.md](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:292).

Other checks: D211’s ledger-blind extraction and D207’s narrowest-home placement are accurately represented for v38 ([decisions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2680), [placement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2630)). The diagram’s “top ten by wording” is an oversimplification: exact-key paths can supply the list instead ([matching.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:133)). H6’s worthwhile-to-track test is supported by the standard ([lines 280–287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:280)); its claim that the model failed to apply it remains a diagnosis to prove, not an established cause.

I verified the principal counts and claims cited above, but did **not** independently verify every cost estimate, every census row’s classification, or the claim that all three M2 cases have the stated period-start mechanism. Those remain unverified rather than established errors. No files were edited.
docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-2.txt
**Serious findings remain; the plan is not ready for approval.** For example, the revised gold can still miss a lost required question, and its evidence check can pass when evidence is attached to the wrong question.

1. **The required-question guard covers only selected old cases.** [Plan lines 105–107](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:105) build G2 from questions that opened and G2′ from the existing admission and ACP golds. A required question absent from both the ledger and those selected cases remains invisible. Fixed extraction replay also cannot reveal a candidate the stored extraction never produced. [D212](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2707) records 94 material losses with no corresponding question during the week. The added G2′ improves round 1’s coverage but does not close it.

2. **The evidence-placement check can certify the wrong destination.** [Plan line 132](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:132) checks that a merged candidate’s evidence reaches *the question it was merged into*. If the merge target itself is wrong, that check can pass. It also does not say how evidence dropped before or during writing is counted. The expected destination must come from the independently judged case; [D212](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2705) found three material records under the wrong product question.

3. **G1’s stated material omits evidence the collision judge actually reads.** [Plan line 105](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:105) lists the candidate’s evidence but not the existing question’s evidence. [jev_matching.py lines 64–68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:64) supplies up to two existing evidence quotes per question. Those quotes appear in the stored ACP collision requests, including calls 66 and 236 in the [v38 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/archive.sqlite). A gold reader could therefore label a different case from the one the model saw.

4. **A hard case crosses objects, but the new gold is grouped by object.** [Plan lines 105–106](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:105) add same-object pairs and judge each object’s questions separately. In the [sealed ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite), `v40` asks about power allocation for the southern Ohio site under `chip-export-controls`; `v44` asks about approval of the Pike County campus arrangement under `NVDA`. The [earlier review line 42](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:42) groups them as the same Ohio matter. Whether their outcomes are identical needs source-based judgment; the current gold construction does not require that judgment.

5. **The ACP example still claims a contradiction and cause that the records do not prove.** [Plan lines 13–20](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:13) and [line 77](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:77) treat the three verdicts as mutually inconsistent and the standard’s two sentences as conflicting. The [signed standard lines 316–319](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:316) do contain both sentences, as the plan says, but whether they conflict depends on whether the quantities are parts of one program or independently answerable quantities. In the [v38 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/archive.sqlite), call 66 says `v3` is narrower than `v1`; call 236 compares a *different candidate* with both. That warrants investigation, not a logical contradiction by itself. The stored reasons also do not identify which prompt sentence caused the split; the planned model continuation is still needed.

6. **H4’s lean does not name the period conflict it creates.** [Plan line 94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:94) proposes folding a calendar-year question into a fiscal-year question because company quantities use fiscal years. But the example differs in both period and scope, and the [standard lines 274 and 316–317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:274) distinguish one period, the same result, and a later period. The fiscal-year writing rule does not itself establish that these are the same result. H3 names its conflicting separation rule; H4 should likewise present this as an unresolved change to the identity boundary.

7. **The holdout does not have the promised independent disagreement check.** [Plan lines 112–114](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:112) give the holdout to Codex alone but say disagreements go to Alex. With only one labeler, there is no second blind label to reveal disagreements. [AGENTS.md lines 246–251](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:246) require a blind second reader and decisions on disagreements. Separating the labels from the person tuning the prompt is a real improvement, but it does not replace that check.

8. **Some reported mechanisms and counts are still stronger than their evidence.** [Plan lines 8–10 and 81](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:8) state that 22 questions should not exist and call M5 a traced mechanism. Those are the earlier author’s unapproved judgments, and M5 itself says the raw answers have not been checked. Line 81 also says four borderline cases, while the corrected count at line 9 is five. The [earlier gold’s individual rows](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:7) support 23 keep, 12 duplicate, 10 do not open, and five borderline—not the file’s own 24/12/10/4 summary.

I re-ran the corrected census read-only: its outcome totals match the plan—sealed **69 questions, 122 candidates, 39 merges, two successions**; v38 **17, 32, 12 and one**. The verdict-category counts remain provisional because the [script parses reason text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:18), as the plan acknowledges. The signed standard and prompt do both contain the guarantee/financing example. H7’s revised lean preserves D204’s two records, and H3 now names the relevant separation rule and D236. Cost estimates and every individual census classification were not independently verified.

**Yes, serious findings remain**, especially the missing-question and wrong-destination guardrails. No files were edited.

docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-2-interrupted.txt
**Serious findings remain.** The plan’s revised checks can still miss a lost question or a wrong merge, and one central example compares different requests as if they were the same pair.

1. **The gold still has a cross-object blind spot.** [Plan lines 104–105]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:104) add unlisted pairs only for the same object and group G2 by object. In the sealed ledger, `v40` is an Ohio power-allocation question under `chip-export-controls`; `v44` asks about the Pike County arrangement under `NVDA`. The earlier review labels `v44` a duplicate of `v40` ([gold, lines 46–50](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:46)). At `v44`’s creation, `v40` was absent from its recorded candidate list in the [sealed ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite). Whether they are truly the same question needs a source-based decision; the proposed gold would not require that comparison.

2. **The regression checks can still pass a wrong destination.** [Plan lines 129–131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:129) define a new false merge only where the control did **not** merge. If the control merges a candidate correctly into A and the new arm merges it incorrectly into B, that definition misses it. Requiring its evidence to land under B then passes the wrong destination by construction. D207 requires a statement’s appropriate, narrowest home, not merely the destination selected by matching ([D207](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2630)). The independent reading of each merge helps, but the stated acceptance rule needs to compare the chosen destination with the gold.

3. **G2′ catches only the required questions already named in two older sets.** [Plan line 106](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:106) does not require a complete source-derived list for the five ledgers. A required question outside those cases can disappear without failing “必开丢失.” D188 describes a 20-case gold, not a complete reference for this corpus ([D188](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2483)). Fixed extraction also cannot recover a required question that extraction never proposed; that limitation needs to be explicit in the score.

4. **The ACP inconsistency is misidentified.** [Plan lines 16–18 and 76](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:16) say the judge gave opposite answers for the same `v1`/`v3` pair. In the [v38 ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/views.sqlite), match record 6 compared the July 2 candidate that became `v3` with `v1` and said distinct. Record 20 compared a **new July 6 candidate** with both `v1` and `v3` and said it was the same as each. These are different candidate texts and contexts, so they do not prove the same request is inconsistent. The archived answers do give reasons, correcting round 1’s statement, but do not establish which instruction caused them. The quoted guarantee/financing sentence **is** in both the [prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1) and [signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318). The boundary may need clarification; the stored example does not by itself prove that sentence conflicts with D210 or caused the split.

5. **H1’s proposed shortcut is broader than its cited rule.** [Plan line 90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:90) leans toward merging because the same annual-report note checks both amounts. A single document can report two independently moving amounts. The standard requires the *same result* to settle both and also requires scope to distinguish financing from guarantees ([standard, lines 293–294 and 316–319](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:293)). D210’s one-deal-or-programme wording does not establish that sharing a note makes two questions identical ([D210](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2670)).

6. **H2 describes a later question without yet showing how it survives until then.** [Plan lines 91 and 171](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:91) merge a later deadline into an earlier question, then propose opening a successor after expiry. The earlier question keeps its fixed deadline and outcomes ([PRD 06, lines 74–75](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:74)); the present succession path acts when matching a candidate ([code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:284)). The plan leaves the later deadline’s preservation and the event that creates the successor to a future table. Without those, Kyber’s 2028 question can be lost if no new article arrives after 2027.

7. **The holdout repair is incomplete.** [Plan lines 111–113](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:111) give the holdout only one blind labeller, so there is no second independent label with which to identify the “disagreements” it says Alex will decide. The working rule requires a blind second reader and recorded resolution ([AGENTS, lines 246–251](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:246)). Also, the plan does not say how holdout results stay out of subsequent tuning when each arm is measured. The separation of the author from the labels is an improvement, but it does not yet establish that control.

8. **The first-50 summary repeats a source’s arithmetic error.** [Plan lines 8–9 and 80](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:8) report four borderline questions. Counting the actual rows in [the earlier gold](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:15) gives **23 keep, 12 duplicate, 10 do not open, and 5 borderline**; its summary line says 24 and 4. The 22 duplicate-or-do-not-open labels are correct as that reader’s judgments, but they remain unapproved judgments rather than established defects.

I re-ran the corrected census read-only: **122 candidates, 39 merges and 2 successions** for the sealed ledger, and **32 candidates, 12 merges and 1 succession** for v38. The reported evidence counts match the census outputs, subject to the plan’s correct qualification that “same claim” labels are estimates. H3 now names the conflicting milestone rule; H4 is presented as a choice; and H7’s lean preserves D204. I did not independently verify every cost estimate, every archived verdict classification, or every factual claim in the plan.

**Serious findings remain**, chiefly the gold’s coverage and the acceptance checks’ ability to pass a wrong destination. No files were edited.
docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-3.txt
**Serious findings remain.** The revised plan improves the earlier checks, but its required-question gold can still miss a question that matching loses.

1. **The required-question gold cannot be derived completely from the pair labels.** [Plan lines 106–108](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:106) define G1 as comparisons with already-open questions, then claim G2′ can be derived from candidates labelled “a question of its own.” But “a question of its own” is not one of G1’s three listed labels. A candidate with no earlier question to compare has no pair label at all. In the [v38 ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/views.sqlite), match record 2 has two candidates and says `no list` for both. Same-answer candidates can also be coalesced by [identity.py lines 51–63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:51). A regression that loses one can therefore escape the stated G2′ check. The distinction between matching losses and questions extraction never proposed is now correctly stated.

2. **The false-merge guard can be waived by the control.** [Plan lines 132 and 141](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:132) require zero false merges, but also say a threshold the control fails against itself is not used. If that includes the zero-false-merge threshold, an arm could pass after merging away a required question. The working rule says a control that fails its own bar does not make that bar informative; it does not establish that a lost question is acceptable ([AGENTS.md lines 299–301](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:299)). The plan needs to say how individual losses remain a stop condition when the control is noisy.

3. **The ACP cause and contradiction are still asserted as facts.** The correction at [plan lines 16–20](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:16) correctly says the July 2 and July 6 comparisons used different candidates. But [lines 78 and 183](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:78) still say the verdicts contradict each other, and line 78 says the two rules conflict. The stored reasons support a boundary to investigate, not those conclusions. The guarantee/financing sentence **does** appear in both the [signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318) and [collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1); neither establishes that it caused this split.

4. **H1’s lean adds an unacknowledged exception to the signed rule.** [Plan line 92](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:92) says different transactions should be separate when counterparties or assets differ. The [signed standard line 319](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:319) instead says a transaction judged to be part of an already-open amount-based programme merges into it. The lean names D210 and the two standard sentences, but does not identify this specific conflict. Whether the examples are actually part of one programme still needs source-based judgment.

5. **M2 presents an unverified count as a traced mechanism.** [Plan line 79](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:79) says all three were blocked by conflicting identity keys, while also saying the third was not checked. The [census script lines 25–33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:25) counts a new decision whenever *any* peer received a `same_question` verdict; it does not establish why that candidate opened. The code has several conditions for merging or succession ([jev_matching.py lines 310–325](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:310)). Two cases are supported by the earlier review; the third remains untraced.

6. **The comparison-list description is factually wrong for the sealed run.** [Plan line 34](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:34) says exact-key matches are combined with word-search results and capped at ten. In [matching.py lines 133–150](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:133), a key match takes a separate branch; word search is the fallback. Then [jev_matching.py line 221](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:221) adds shown questions after the cap. Sealed match record 413 contains more than ten comparisons. This matters when attributing M3 to a question being absent from the comparison.

7. **H5’s stated change does not cover every use of the start date.** [Plan lines 96 and 154](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:96) propose removing the start date from conflict checking. It also enters the automatic identity key ([identity.py lines 18–32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:18)), list construction ([matching.py lines 122–144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:122)), and succession eligibility ([jev_matching.py lines 95–101](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:95)). The proposed change may fix the two observed cases, but the plan has not yet shown it removes the counted class or preserves the other paths.

The round-two repairs for cross-object comparisons, evidence destinations, two blind holdout readers, H4’s stated period conflict, and preserving H2’s later deadline are present in the revised plan. H3 now names its conflicting separation rule; H6 and H7 clearly present their leans as choices.

I reran the census read-only and verified the reported **69/122/39/2** and **17/32/12/1** question/candidate/merge/succession counts, plus the four displayed evidence counts. I also checked the earlier gold’s individual labels: **23 keep, 12 duplicate, 10 do not open, five borderline**. The verdict categories remain provisional because the script parses reason text. I did not independently verify every quotation, archived classification, estimate, or factual claim in the plan.

**Serious findings remain.**
docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-4.txt
**Serious findings remain.** For example, the revised gold can still miss a required successor question, and it can also demand that a distinct but trivial question be opened.

1. **Serious — the required-question check excludes successors.** [Plan lines 107–109]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:107) give “successor to X” its own G1 label, but derive G2′ only from candidates labelled “a question of its own.” A required later-period question can therefore disappear without failing G2′. The sealed ledger’s `v68`, the 2027 CPU-revenue successor to `v24`, is a concrete case; the [earlier review, line 92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:92) identifies it. The [standard, line 317]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:317) treats the next period as a successor.

2. **Serious — “distinct” is being equated with “must open.”** [Plan lines 107–109]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:107) give every candidate an identity label but no “distinct, yet not worth opening” label; G2′ then makes every “question of its own” mandatory. The [standard, lines 281–287]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:281), separately requires worth and independent investment meaning. For example, the earlier reader marked the distinct RTX 50 SUPER release question `v19` as “do not open” ([gold, line 25]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:25)); that label is not Alex-approved, but it shows the distinction the gold must be able to express. As written, G1, G2 and G2′ can disagree.

3. **Serious — the proposed holdout includes cases already read.** [Plan line 115]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:115) uses the sealed run’s second half as the holdout. Its first 50 questions were already labelled in the earlier review, including `v48` on August 4 and `v49` on August 7 in the stored ledger; the [earlier gold, lines 54–55]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:54) records those labels. The earlier review also discusses `v68` on August 28 ([line 92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:92)). Blind readers help, but a date split alone does not make these cases unseen. [AGENTS.md, lines 246–255]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:246) requires a held-out set no arm has seen.

4. **Serious — the evidence guard still omits successor evidence.** [Plan line 135]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:135) checks evidence for **merged** candidates. It does not check evidence for a newly opened successor. The sealed run’s `v68` was opened without the evidence that prompted it, as documented in the [earlier review, lines 90–92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:90). That loss can pass the stated check. The revised expected-destination check is an improvement for merges.

5. **Serious — H2’s key future transition is not exercised by the stated replay.** [Plan lines 94 and 176]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:94) propose opening a later question when the earlier deadline expires. The stored Kyber example’s earlier deadline is December 31, 2027, while the sealed replay ends in August 2026. The [planned measurements]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:142) do not state a check that advances through that expiry. A replay could pass while the deferred successor never opens.

6. **Factual — the shared-quantity comparison list is capped.** [Plan line 34]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:34) says the list includes *all* questions with the same theme and quantity. [matching.py, lines 137–140]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:137) caps that list at `top_n`. Separately, [jev_matching.py, lines 104–118]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:104) searches the family to select the shared-quantity destination. Those are different operations.

7. **Wording-level — section 12 still says the ACP verdicts were changed to “mutually contradictory.”** [Plan line 202]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:202) conflicts with the corrected account at [lines 16–20]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:16), which correctly says the candidates differed and the cause is unproven.

The ACP “guarantee and financing” sentence is in both the [signed standard, line 318]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318) and the [collision prompt]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1). The revised plan no longer claims it caused the split or that the two standard sentences necessarily conflict. H1–H5 now name their relevant boundaries as choices; H7’s lean retains D204’s two records. The census totals match the recorded outputs, but its verdict categories remain provisional because the [script parses reason text]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:18). I did not independently verify every archived verdict, quotation or cost estimate.

**Yes, serious findings remain.**
docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-5.txt
**Serious findings remain.** The new per-candidate check closes several round-4 gaps, but it can still let an unworthy question open without failing acceptance. For example, a candidate marked “distinct but not worth opening” can be opened and still match the stated identity check.

1. **Serious — the gold records whether a question is worth opening, but acceptance does not check that outcome.** [Plan lines 113–116](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:113) add a separate “worth opening” field. Yet [lines 146–152](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:146) compare identity, destination and evidence; none of the three failure classes catches a distinct, unworthy candidate that opens. The signed standard independently requires the question to be worth pursuing ([standard lines 281–287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:281)). The earlier reader’s RTX 50 SUPER example shows why “distinct” and “worth opening” need separate outcomes, although that label has not been approved by Alex ([gold line 25](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:25)).

   The related statement that all D188 “must not open” cases should be set aside is too broad: [plan line 121](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:121) cites the gold check, but it says many such cases remain valid and identifies the subset that should become “propose, then merge” cases ([gold check lines 62–65](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/gold-check.md:62)).

2. **Serious — the cited pinned-replay script is not ready to prove the proposed measurement.** [Plan line 144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:144) points to the existing script for holding extraction fixed while changing matching. That script is a historical v25/v26 study: it uses hard-coded windows and configurations, removes group references, and returns an empty extraction answer when a stored answer is missing ([script lines 20–21, 52–66 and 101–108](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-26-thesis-admission-v12/pinned_matching_study.py:20)). A missing input can therefore look like a run with no candidates or evidence. The script needs an adapted, verified replay before its results can support the plan’s claim.

3. **Serious — comparing cross-object or cross-type pairs is not enough to merge them.** The plan adds cross-object pairs to G1 and leaves the comparison scope for arm D to be decided ([plan lines 123 and 172](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:123)). The sealed ledger contains the plan’s example: `v42` is under `chip-export-controls`, while `v44` is under `NVDA`; the earlier reader considered them duplicates of the Ohio power question ([earlier gold lines 46–50](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:46)). That judgment remains subject to the gold review. Even if a judge calls the pair the same, the current writer’s check turns a cross-object merge back into a new question ([matching.py lines 295–305](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:295)). Different measure types also conflict in [identity.py lines 35–38](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:35). The plan does not yet trace these checks in arm D’s new-path table, so expanding the judge’s list alone cannot establish that those cases are fixed.

4. **Serious — evidence linked directly to an existing question is outside the stated per-candidate check.** G1 records evidence brought by each candidate ([plan line 116](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:116)). But the pipeline also processes evidence linked directly to existing questions, without a candidate ([matching.py lines 151–155](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:151); [PRD 06 line 163](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:163)). If changing question identity changes where such evidence goes, the proposed per-candidate comparison does not state that it will inspect it. This matters to question-identity measurement: the earlier audit found material records under the wrong product question ([D212](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2705)).

The round-4 fixes do address the named successor cases: G1 now distinguishes successors from distinct unworthy questions, includes successor evidence, and adds an expiry test. The new September holdout also avoids the already-read sealed cases. Those fixes do **not** yet establish that the broader “complete outcome” rule covers every outcome, for the reasons above.

I verified the displayed census totals against the stored census output, and the ACP sentence is present in both the [signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318) and [collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1). The revised plan correctly says its role in the split is unproven. The leans now identify the principal standard boundaries as choices. I did **not** independently verify every archived verdict, quotation, cost estimate or factual claim in the plan; the verdict census still relies on reason-text parsing ([census script lines 18–37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:18)).

**Yes, serious findings remain.**
docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/review-6.txt
**Serious findings remain.** The per-candidate check can catch a false merge or a lost required question when that candidate is in G1, but it does not yet cover every relevant outcome or every evidence loss.

1. **Serious — the round-5 fix still misses directly filed evidence that both arms lose.** The plan checks direct evidence only when its destination changes against the control ([plan line 157](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:157)). If both runs drop the same required statement, there is no changed destination to inspect. Placement can drop an unfiled statement before matching ([placement.py line 53](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/placement.py:53); [extraction.py line 608](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/extraction.py:608)). The earlier audit recorded a case where the revenue page was present but a revenue statement was not filed there ([audit line 49](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-28-retention-audit/results.md:49)). That audit’s labels are not signed gold, but the example shows the gap. Required direct evidence needs an expected outcome independent of the control.

2. **Serious — the holdout does not specify how its existing questions come into being.** The plan says to run only extraction and placement for the new September week ([plan lines 133–134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:133)). Placement needs the questions open at that article’s turn; matching and writing create those questions ([round.py lines 103–130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/round.py:103)). Without a defined starting ledger and a way to advance it, the holdout cannot establish the comparisons and evidence destinations G1 is meant to judge.

3. **Serious — the claimed closed set omits a real refusal outcome.** G1 says every ledger candidate is either its own question, the same as another, or a successor ([plan lines 107–115](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:107)). In the read-only [v38 ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/views.sqlite), item `gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57` has two candidates with the same question text; `n1` was refused because its object was outside the research universe. The product contract permits that refusal ([PRD 06 line 178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:178)). “Same question” and “not worth opening” do not record why this candidate was correctly refused. Thus the round-4 structural rule covers the named successor cases, but is **not yet a complete closed set**.

4. **Serious — re-sampling placement makes the matching comparison ambiguous.** The plan says score changes come only from matching, while also re-sending placement because its list changes ([plan lines 144–147](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:144)). Placement is a model call ([placement.py lines 59–70](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/placement.py:59)); a different placement answer can change evidence even with the same question list. The earlier audit explicitly says duplicate pages causing placement misses was **not proven** ([audit line 66](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-28-retention-audit/results.md:66)). The comparison therefore needs to distinguish matching’s effect from placement variation before attributing a score change.

The central source checks support several corrections already made. The guarantee/financing sentence is in both the [signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318) and the [collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1); its causing the ACP split remains unproven, as the plan now says. H1–H7 identify their principal changes as choices, and H7’s lean agrees with [D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2609). The displayed census totals match the saved outputs, but verdict classes still depend on reason-text parsing ([census script line 25](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/census/admission_census.py:25)). I did not independently verify every archived verdict, quote, estimate, or factual claim.

**Yes, serious findings remain.**
